      {
        "name": "📝 Week notes · 2",
        "value": "Looking at the whole week rather than any single day, the pattern is clearer than it was a month ago. The hard days are the ones where work runs late and the evening gets compressed, and the routine is the first thing to go when that happens. What has changed is that a missed night no longer turns into a missed week: the next morning he notices, writes it down, and starts again without making a story out of it. That recovery is the real progress, more than any streak. The open question is how to protect the evenings when the end-of-month deadline lands, because the calendar will not get lighter on its own. A friend asking about that directly, and offering a walk or a meal on one of those evenings, would probably help more than encouragement about the numbers. He tends to say he is fine when asked in general terms, so a specific question works better than a general one. He also does better with a plan made a day ahead than with a last-minute invitation, so suggesting a time early in the week is kinder than tex"
